<h1>polynomial</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
(1) Any function which is the sum of constants times powers of the argument: f(x)=&Sigma;<sub>i=0</sub><sup>k</sup> c<sub>i</sub>x<sup>p<sub>i</sub></sup>. (2) In complexity theory, the measure of computation, m(n) (usually execution time or memory space), is bounded by a polynomial function of the problem size, n.  More formally m(n) = <a href="bigOnotation.html"><em>O(n<sup>k</sup>)</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="logarithmic.html"><em>logarithmic</em></a>, <a href="linear.html"><em>linear</em></a>, <a href="exponential.html"><em>exponential</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Strictly speaking, a linear function is a polynomial with the highest exponent being 1, and a logarithmic function is a polynomial with fractional exponents.  However polynomial usually means exponents greater than 1.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

